% ============================================================================
% phoneme-deletion — removing one sound from a word to form a new word, a
% SIXTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all five prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING, `syllable-count.adj` recalls a
% SYLLABLE COUNT, `initial-sound.adj` recalls a BEGINNING sound,
% `onset-rime.adj` recalls an ONSET/RIME split, and `phoneme-substitution.adj`
% recalls the new word formed by SWAPPING one sound for another. This library
% recalls what happens when you DELETE (remove) one sound from a word,
% grounding the same phonemic-awareness continuum's deletion skill as a pure
% lookup: `phoneme_deletion(original_word, removed_sound, new_word)`. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? phoneme_deletion(bike, k, $New)   % by
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `onset-rime.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `phoneme_deletion(original_word, removed_sound, new_word)` carrying the
% Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the new word WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on any deletion not the one the cited page
% walks through. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the new word and recall
% which word it came from and which sound was removed:
%
%     ? phoneme_deletion($Orig, $Sound, by)   % bike, k
%
% The one deletion this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     original_word   removed_sound   new_word
%     -------------   -------------   --------
%     bike            k               by
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj` and `phoneme-substitution.adj` already cite, a
% different section of it (its "Deleting Sounds" demonstration) —
% WebFetch-verified TWICE for consistency before writing. The exact spans,
% composed together the same way `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s row is (two
% distinct sentences from the same cited section, each stating one fact):
%
%   "I will change 'bike' to 'by'."
%     (states the transformation: bike -> by)
%   "The last sound in 'bike' is /k/."
%     (states which sound was removed: k)
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s and
% `syllable-count.adj`'s sibling sections of this page already earned.
% Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE deletion the cited page walks through
% step by step — honestly narrow (mirroring `phoneme-substitution.adj`'s and
% `onset-rime.adj`'s precedent) rather than inventing a deletion the source
% does not demonstrate. Also checked this same page's "suntan" -> "sunset"
% demonstration before writing this table: that example changes a whole
% SYLLABLE, not a single phoneme, so it is a distinct skill and does NOT
% belong in either this table or `phoneme-substitution.adj` — left as a
% future candidate for its own syllable-substitution table if revisited. A
% future slice can add a second deletion once a comparably clean citation is
% found. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table phoneme_deletion {
    columns original_word, removed_sound, new_word

    row (bike, k, by)

    source "I will change 'bike' to 'by'."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
